course: dictionary
lesson: 6159
title: One word, several meanings
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: One word can have more than one meaning, and the context tells you which one is meant. «A sausage at a barbecue is mostly minced pork, and pork is meat from a pig.» The words pork and pig show that minced means cut into very small pieces.
explain: Look at how the word is used. If mince comes after words like «to», it is probably an action, as in «mince the garlic». If it is a thing you could eat or cook, it is probably the noun, meat cut into tiny pieces.
explain: Some phrases have a meaning of their own. «She didn't mince her words» does not mean she chopped up words. It means she said exactly what she thought, even if it was not polite. Learn phrases like this as a whole.
fact: Mince is meat cut into very small pieces, used for burgers, spaghetti bolognese and cottage pie. In the US and Canada it is called «ground beef» or «hamburger meat».
fact: To mince food means to cut it into very small pieces.
fact: To mince is also to walk with small, quick, fussy steps.
fact: «Not mince your words» means to say something directly, even if it is not polite.
fact: In Scotland, «mince and tatties» is a dinner of minced meat and potatoes, and «talking mince» means talking nonsense.
exercise: «A sausage at a barbecue is mostly minced pork, and pork is meat from a pig.» What does minced mean here? | Ready to be eaten whole | Burnt black on the barbecue | Wrapped in a bun | *Cut into very small pieces
exercise: Mia's mum says, «Mince the garlic, please.» What does mince mean? | Put it in a sandwich | Walk quickly to the shop | Cook it for a very long time | *Cut it into very small pieces
exercise: «She didn't mince her words when she told him the truth.» What does this mean? | She said it very quietly | She cut the words into small bits | *She spoke bluntly and directly | She wrote it down on paper
test:
«She walked across the hall with small, quick, fussy steps.» Which word from the sentence shows how she walked? Think about the verb form: mincing means walking with small, quick, fussy steps. | *Mincing | Running | Jumping | Sliding
Ruby sees «mince and tatties» on a Scottish menu. What is it? | A fancy cake | *A dinner of minced meat and potatoes | A fruit pudding | A type of fast walk
exam:
«Mince pies» are a Christmas treat, and today they are filled with what? | Minced beef | *Fruit, not meat | Cheese and onion | Fish
